%I A398918 #32 Nov 24 2026 15:32:17
%S A398918 1,9,46,323,2554,20912,176718,1531317,13510251,120907765
%N A398918 Number of primes p <= 10^n having a Fibonacci primitive root.
%C A398918 A Fibonacci primitive root modulo a prime p is a primitive root g modulo p such that g^2 == g + 1 (mod p); the primes having one are listed in A003147.
%C A398918 Shanks (1972) conjectured that a(n)/A006880(n) tends to (27/38)*A = 0.2657054465..., where A = 0.3739558136... is Artin's constant (A005596). Sander (1990) proved this under the assumption of the Riemann hypothesis for the fields Q(phi^(1/k), zeta_(2k)), k >= 1, where phi = (1+sqrt(5))/2 and zeta_(2k) is a primitive (2k)-th root of unity.
%C A398918 a(n)/A006880(n) = 0.2659096..., 0.2657865..., 0.2657012..., 0.2657006... for n = 7..10. Shanks expected the convergence to the limit to be "mostly from above"; a(n)/A006880(n) is above the limit for n = 2, 3, 5, 6, 7, 8 and below it for n = 1, 4, 9, 10.
%H A398918 J. W. Sander, <a href="https://www.fq.math.ca/Scanned/28-1/sander.pdf">On Fibonacci primitive roots</a>, Fibonacci Quarterly, Vol. 28, No. 1 (1990), pp. 79-80.
%H A398918 Daniel Shanks, <a href="https://www.fq.math.ca/Scanned/10-2/shanks-a.pdf">Fibonacci primitive roots</a>, <a href="https://www.fq.math.ca/Scanned/10-2/shanks-b.pdf">end of article</a>, Fibonacci Quarterly, Vol. 10, No. 2 (1972), pp. 163-168, 181.
%F A398918 Conjecture: a(n) ~ (27/38)*A005596*A006880(n) (Shanks, 1972); this holds under the generalized Riemann hypothesis (Sander, 1990).
%e A398918 a(2) = 9 because the primes p <= 100 having a Fibonacci primitive root are 5, 11, 19, 31, 41, 59, 61, 71 and 79.
%o A398918 (PARI) isfpr(p) = if(p==5, return(1)); if(kronecker(5, p)!=1, return(0)); my(s=sqrt(Mod(5, p))); znorder((1+s)/2)==p-1 || znorder((1-s)/2)==p-1;
%o A398918 a(n) = my(c=0); forprime(p=2, 10^n, c += isfpr(p)); c;
%Y A398918 Cf. A003147, A005596, A006880, A106535, A384490, A402373.
%K A398918 nonn,hard,more,new
%O A398918 1,2
%A A398918 _Patryk Majda_, Nov 17 2026
